{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1660 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "689b12453ed75a5b1c57bbe1aa0e8747f76ccb9e13e743f216e933d88805c98f" :edb/db-before-t 1659 :edb/db-after-t 1660 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534972 50 #inst "2026-10-08T12:42:13.812Z" 13194139534972 true] [17592186179504 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/c4d5f211c3bf3d306c6dc14205311c60eeaaf55de89f8af571ec0fa9e5add8a6.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = xy?$" 13194139534972 true] [17592186179504 1100 "Which of the above diagrams could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = xy$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/87979b85b4ea12aacf6bd158adc3fcb2.png)" 13194139534972 false] [17592186179504 1145 "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -1$, we get $xy = -1$, which gives the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 1x$\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 0$, we get $xy = 0$, that is, the lines $y=0$ and $x=0$\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 1$, we get $xy =1$, that is, the hyperbola $y=\\dfrac 1x$\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -2$, we get $xy =-2$, that is, the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 2x$\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/5691898f126e352c214c8c48bb94c312.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the upper half-plane $y > 0$, and increases as we move from right-to-left in the lower half-plane $y < 0$.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/54fea7979eb64dee5d535a276bd80c43.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/55af8d7d2226cd79132c79e19bbce747.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III." 13194139534972 false] [17592186179504 1145 "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -1,$ we get $xy = -1,$ which gives the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 0,$ we get $xy = 0,$ that is, the lines $y=0$ and $x=0$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 1,$ we get $xy =1,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -2,$ we get $xy =-2,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 2x$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/f95c7ab30b7b02d67ceec039792e649a900dbece2896cc1e63341fcba9fc794f.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the upper half-plane $y > 0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the lower half-plane $y < 0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/3d6be27e083bb2e8b508cca31671e5cb4ea4e39b366a99f1dd194a5ea8ffcd3b.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/996b5678396b4e9a91a4c55697442895331a7a6e4f8ec3eed550ea86845e44f4.png)\n\n       \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III." 13194139534972 true] [17592186179506 1100 "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = e^{2x-3y}$." 13194139534972 false] [17592186179506 1100 "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = e^{2x-3y}.$" 13194139534972 true] [17592186179506 1145 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f$.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(e^{2x - 3y}) \\\\\n&= 2e^{2x - 3y} \\\\\nf_{y}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(e^{2x - 3y}) \\\\\n&=-3e^{2x - 3y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( x , y ) & = {f}_{x} ( x , y ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + {f}_{y} ( x , y ) \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = 2 {e}^{2 x - 3 y} \\, \\mathbf{i} - 3 {e}^{2 x - 3 y} \\, \\mathbf{j} .\\end{array}$$" 13194139534972 false] [17592186179506 1145 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y}\\,i-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534972 true] [17592186179511 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/f9de4a1c2dfb6b8939b2440930eefb8b7020947bd7b943021214adf4cbfce735.png)\n\n    \n\nWhich of the above diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = 2y^2-x^2?$" 13194139534972 true] [17592186179511 1100 "Which of the above diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = 2y^2-x^2$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/ae5318226e1538c2b166e0591310b036.png)" 13194139534972 false] [17592186179511 1145 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f$.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(2y^{2} - x^{2}) \\\\\n&=-2x \\\\\nf_{y}(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(2y^{2} - x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( x , y ) & = {f}_{x} ( x , y ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + {f}_{y} ( x , y ) \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = - 2 x \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 y \\, \\mathbf{j} .\\end{array}$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1)$, $(-2,1)$, and $(1,-1)$.\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f$.\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 2 , 1 ) & = - 2 \\cdot  2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\cdot  1 \\, \\mathbf{j} = - 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( - 2 , 1 ) & = - 2 \\cdot  ( - 2 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\cdot  1 \\, \\mathbf{j} = 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 1 , - 1 ) & = - 2 \\cdot  1 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\cdot  ( - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{j} = - 2 \\, \\mathbf{i} - 4 \\, \\mathbf{j}\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3692/Source/Images/5cb2c61d730527746c2d42d5a8af642e.png)" 13194139534972 false] [17592186179511 1145 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2x\\,i+4y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(-2,1),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =-2\\cdot 2\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=-4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,1) & =-2\\cdot (-2)\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =-2\\cdot 1\\,i+4\\cdot (-1)\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/b88c62b154cecaace206248b62f72b8aa1f71662c0427adfbbabd5c2b4738a6b.png)" 13194139534972 true] [17592186179642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323803 13194139534972 true] [17592186179642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323810 13194139534972 true] [17592186179642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323817 13194139534972 true] [17592186179642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323824 13194139534972 true] [17592186179644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323747 13194139534972 true] [17592186179644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323754 13194139534972 true] [17592186179644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323761 13194139534972 true] [17592186179644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323768 13194139534972 true] [17592186179649 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323775 13194139534972 true] [17592186179649 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323782 13194139534972 true] [17592186179649 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323789 13194139534972 true] [17592186179649 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323796 13194139534972 true] [17592186179896 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534972 true] [17592186179896 1100 "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y, z) = x\\ln (z - 2y)$." 13194139534972 false] [17592186179896 1100 "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y).$" 13194139534972 true] [17592186179896 1145 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{z-2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i-\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534972 true] [17592186179902 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534972 true] [17592186179902 1100 "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} + y^{2}$?" 13194139534972 false] [17592186179902 1100 "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}?$" 13194139534972 true] [17592186179902 1145 "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png)" 13194139534972 true] [17592186179904 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534972 true] [17592186179904 1100 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y) = \\frac{x^{3} - 2y^{2}}{4}$?" 13194139534972 false] [17592186179904 1100 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}?$" 13194139534972 true] [17592186179904 1145 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,-3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{2}\\,i-2\\,j=3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-3) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-(-3)\\,j=3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-\\,j=3\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png)" 13194139534972 true] [17592186323747 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323748 13194139534972 true] [17592186323747 1089 "q-122596" 13194139534972 true] [17592186323747 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534972 true] [17592186323747 1100 "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}.$" 13194139534972 true] [17592186323747 1113 #uuid "a300dd68-88af-507e-9066-e4497e289afb" 13194139534972 true] [17592186323747 1145 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534972 true] [17592186323748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323749 13194139534972 true] [17592186323748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323750 13194139534972 true] [17592186323748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323751 13194139534972 true] [17592186323748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323752 13194139534972 true] [17592186323748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323753 13194139534972 true] [17592186323748 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534972 true] [17592186323748 1041 "selection" 13194139534972 true] [17592186323748 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323749 13194139534972 true] [17592186323748 1110 #uuid "1c8bc395-67b6-55b7-880b-49b60e71b422" 13194139534972 true] [17592186323749 1034 "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k" 13194139534972 true] [17592186323749 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534972 true] [17592186323749 111